 Infrastructure as Code (Production Level)

For 2–4 years experience, interviewers expect you to design reusable, secure, and scalable IaC, not just write basic Terraform code.

🔹 Terraform Interview Questions (2–4 yrs)

• What is Infrastructure as Code (IaC)?
• Why Terraform over CloudFormation / ARM templates?
• Terraform architecture
• Terraform init, plan, apply — what happens internally?
• What is Terraform state and why is it important?
• Local state vs remote state
• How do you store Terraform state securely?
• What is state locking and why is it needed?
• What are Terraform providers?
• What are Terraform modules?
• How do you create reusable modules?
• Variables vs locals
• count vs for_each
• How do you manage multiple environments in Terraform?
• How do you handle secrets in Terraform?
• Terraform workspaces — when to use and when not?
• How do you import existing infrastructure into Terraform?
• How do you destroy resources safely?
• How do you perform rollback using Terraform?

🔹 Advanced Terraform Concepts

• Backend configuration (S3 + DynamoDB)
• Terraform dependency graph
• Lifecycle rules
• Prevent accidental deletion (prevent_destroy)
• Handling drift in infrastructure
• Terraform with CI/CD pipelines
• Terraform security best practices
• Terraform vs Terragrunt
• Managing large Terraform codebases
🔹 Real-World Terraform Scenarios

• Terraform apply failed midway — what will you do?
• State file got corrupted — recovery steps
• Resource created manually in cloud — how do you manage it?
• Multiple engineers running Terraform — how do you avoid conflicts?
• How do you review Terraform changes before applying?

💡 Interview Tip:
If you say:
👉 “We use remote state with locking, modular Terraform, and CI/CD-based applies”
you already sound like a strong 2–4 years
DevOps engineer.
